Hydrocele
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Painless scrotal swelling, Brilliant transillumination, Palpable cystic mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-018
Reason for Visit: Sudden panic and severe psychological distress after noting a massive increase in scrotal size, requiring immediate assessment to rule out an incarcerated hernia or rapidly growing testicular malignancy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient in a state of acute anxiety regarding a markedly enlarged, heavy scrotum that worsened following physical exertion. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out a strangulated inguinoscrotal hernia and provide immediate diagnostic clarity via physical examination and transillumination, effectively preventing a highly distressing and unnecessary emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My testicle is the size of a tennis ball, it feels incredibly heavy, and I am terrified it is advanced cancer."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a gradual, insidious onset of painless unilateral scrotal swelling over the past six months. Tonight, following an episode of heavy lifting and prolonged standing, they noted a sensation of intense heaviness and a dragging feeling in the scrotum, prompting acute anxiety. They deny any acute, sharp pain, dysuria, fever, or trauma. They have not noticed any changes in bowel habits or vomiting. The swelling is entirely painless but highly uncomfortable due to its sheer mass and the mechanical drag on the spermatic cord.
Functional Status: Independent, but walking with an altered gait to accommodate the scrotal swelling.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety regarding malignancy; otherwise low social risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Anxious, frequently checking the affected area, but appearing well without systemic signs of illness.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Examination reveals a large, palpable, smooth, tense, cystic mass surrounding the right testicle. The examiner is unable to palpate the right testicle separate from the swelling. The mass demonstrates brilliant transillumination when a focused light source is applied, glowing uniformly red. There is a distinct sensation of the swelling being positioned anterior to the testicle. The overlying scrotal skin is normal, without erythema or warmth. There is a lack of fluctuance varying with Valsalva manoeuvre, indicating a non-communicating variant. Expected investigation results will show an anechoic fluid collection surrounding the anterolateral aspect of the testicle without solid septations on formal scrotal ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hydrocele - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Incarcerated Inguinal Hernia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the brilliant transillumination of the mass, the ability to get above the swelling at the external inguinal ring, and normal bowel sounds.

Testicular Tumour - Ruled out/unlikely because the mass is distinctly cystic and transilluminates brightly, rather than presenting as a firm, solid, opaque nodule.

Epididymo-orchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of local tenderness, erythema, and systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe acute distress secondary to a large, newly bothersome scrotal swelling. The critical objective of the urgent after-hours visit was to perform a definitive physical examination to differentiate a benign fluid collection from a surgical emergency (incarcerated hernia) or an oncological crisis (solid testicular mass). The clinical findings of a smooth, painless, brightly transilluminating mass that obscures the testicle are pathognomonic for a simple hydrocele. Performing this assessment immediately alleviated the patient's severe panic and safely diverted them from an inappropriate emergency department presentation, completely fulfilling the criteria for an urgent primary care intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical examination and transillumination test, demonstrating the results directly to the patient to provide immediate, definitive reassurance that the mass is fluid, not solid cancer. Generated a referral for a routine, non-urgent scrotal ultrasound to formally document the fluid collection.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the mechanical dragging discomfort, the patient is advised to wear supportive, fitted briefs rather than loose boxer shorts. Experiencing immediate relief from the physical support will strongly reinforce the habit of wearing appropriate undergarments, mitigating the primary symptomatic complaint while awaiting formal surgical review.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in two weeks to review the ultrasound report and discuss potential elective urological referral for drainage if the mass remains bothersome.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review is required if the scrotum suddenly becomes exquisitely painful, turns red and hot, or if the patient develops severe abdominal pain and vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical exam strongly suggests a harmless collection of fluid, but a routine ultrasound is required to confirm this 100%.
Clinical Course: The fluid collection is benign and will not turn into cancer, but it may gradually increase in size over time, requiring a minor day procedure to drain it if it becomes too heavy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the swelling suddenly becomes very painful, hot to the touch, or if you start vomiting and cannot pass gas, you must go immediately to the Emergency Department as a hernia could have developed.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A hydrocele is simply a buildup of normal lubricating fluid in the sac that surrounds the testicle. Because light shines completely through it, we know it is clear fluid and not a solid tumor or a piece of intestine.
Actionable Steps: Wear supportive, tight underwear to lift the weight and reduce the dragging pain. Attend the routine ultrasound appointment next week. Stop continuously checking the area, as this increases anxiety without changing the diagnosis.

